 | `D-49` | 2026-09-15 | **The frontend runs unprivileged nginx on port 8080**, `nginxinc/nginx-unprivileged:alpine` as uid 101, rather than standard `nginx:alpine` on port 80 | Binding port 80 requires root. Moving to 8080 lets the whole container process run unprivileged, which is what makes "every container runs non-root" true without exception rather than true for two of three. `/usr/share/nginx/html` is chowned to 101 so the entrypoint can write `env.js` without privilege | `04` §1 §4, `W-49`, `W-59` |
 | ~~`D-44`~~ | 2026-09-13 | ~~**RabbitMQ stands in for Azure Service Bus in the local stack.** Production remains Service Bus (`D-10`) — this is a laptop substitute only~~ — **superseded by `D-50`, 2026-09-19.** Production is Storage Queue, which Azurite emulates faithfully, so the local stand-in is Azurite rather than RabbitMQ | No faithful local emulator exists with acceptable licence terms. `W-52` builds a queue abstraction, so local and production differ by one adapter, which is the point of having the abstraction. The alternative, a Postgres-backed queue, tests nothing about real queue behaviour | `04` §6, `W-02` |
 | `D-50` | 2026-09-19 | **Azure Storage Queue replaces Azure Service Bus for the job queue.** Supersedes `D-44` | A private endpoint on Service Bus is **Premium-tier only** — Microsoft: *"This feature is supported with the premium tier"* — at roughly ten times Standard, which `D-19` scale (10 tenants x 100 employees) does not justify. Storage Queue takes a private endpoint on the Standard tier, so the queue satisfies `05` §4 — *"Only Front Door is public. Nothing else has a public endpoint"* — at no extra cost, in the storage account that already exists for documents. The alternative considered and rejected was leaving Service Bus on a public endpoint gated by Entra ID RBAC alone. **Accepted losses:** no topics or subscriptions, no message sessions and therefore no guaranteed ordering, no scheduled messages, and 64 KB per message rather than 256 KB. Founder judged the ordering loss unrealistic at this scale; the consequence is that **`W-52` handles idempotency in code rather than relying on broker ordering** | `04` §6, `05` §3, `D-10`, `D-44`, `W-51`, `W-52` |
+| `D-51` | 2026-09-19 | **Azure Front Door Standard, not Premium**, one profile for all three environments | Premium is roughly ten times the base cost, which `D-19` scale does not justify. Two capabilities are given up and both matter: **managed rule sets** (the OWASP Default Rule Set is Premium-only), so the WAF carries custom rules only — a per-IP rate limit and a canary rule; and **Private Link origins**, which is why origin protection falls to `D-54`. `W-64` re-tests the perimeter and is the place to revisit this | `05` §3, `D-19`, `W-51`, `W-64` |
+| `D-52` | 2026-09-19 | **Postgres is reached by a private endpoint, not delegated-subnet virtual network integration** | The two networking modes are mutually exclusive and fixed when the server is created, and Microsoft does not permit a VNet-integrated server to gain a private endpoint afterwards — so **delegation is a one-way door**. The private endpoint converts the existing server in place, with no dump, recreate and restore; it keeps `publicNetworkAccess` reversible, which is what leaves the subscription Owner a break-glass route to a private database; and it removed the only destructive database step from `W-51`. Being Owner grants no network path on its own — that is a control-plane role | `05` §4, `W-51`, `W-62` |
+| `D-53` | 2026-09-19 | **Key Vault is the single documented exception to "only Front Door is public".** It keeps `publicNetworkAccess: 'Enabled'` with `networkAcls.defaultAction: 'Deny'`, `bypass: 'AzureServices'`, plus a private endpoint | `deploy.sh` writes secrets over the Key Vault **data plane** from CI, before anything exists inside the virtual network. `'Disabled'` removes the endpoint outright and no ACL rule can reopen it, so the deployment that creates the vault could never fill it. `defaultAction: 'Deny'` leaves no open public surface at rest; `deploy.sh` adds its own egress address as a transient `/32` rule and revokes it on every catchable exit path. The alternative — moving all secret generation inside the in-VNet migration job — was considered and not taken. Founder confirmed 2026-09-19 | `05` §4, `W-51`, `W-56` |
+| `D-54` | 2026-09-19 | **Container Apps origins are protected by `ipSecurityRestrictions` against the Front Door backend prefixes. `X-Azure-FDID` header validation is deferred to `W-57`** | Container Apps ingress has **no header-matching rule** — `ipSecurityRestrictions` is the only control the ingress schema offers — and `W-51` is an infrastructure ticket that does not touch `code/`. The first draft of `W-51` specified a header check with nowhere in scope to implement it. Consequence worth stating plainly: until `W-57` lands, the origin boundary is an **IP boundary, not authentication** — anything egressing from a Front Door backend address satisfies it, and the prefix list is re-resolved on every deployment | `05` §4, `D-22`, `D-51`, `W-51`, `W-57` |
 
